// Bugs roadmap BG205: the C runtime's p_socket_poll waited with select()
// on POSIX, so an fd of FD_SETSIZE (1024 on Linux) or more was FD_SET past
// the stack fd_set (a server with more than 1024 descriptors corrupted its
// stack on every poll), and a negative fd wrote before it. Now
// (compiler/codegen_c.fd p_socket_poll) POSIX waits with poll(), as the
// WASI branch did: any fd is fine, a negative or closed one gives -1 (an
// error), as Windows' select gives for something that is no socket.
// Windows keeps select (its fd_set is a list of sockets, no fd range).
// What fails today is the POSIX build (line 1 printed 0, and fd 1500 wrote
// past the set): scripts/cross-test.sh x86_64-linux runs it there.
//@ backends: ccompile
//
// Expected output:
// -1
// -1
// -1

fn main() -> i64 {
    println(p_socket_poll(0 - 1, 0, 0));
    println(p_socket_poll(1500, 0, 1));
    println(p_socket_poll(1500, 0, 0));
    return 0;
}
